1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,660
Okay, GI pathology, the very first section that we're going to talk about is oral cavity.

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:08,120
The first lesion that we want to discuss is cleft, lip, and palate.

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:12,080
This is a full thickness defect of the lip and or the palate.

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,080
Usually a cleft, lip, and palate will occur together.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:23,260
What's important here and perhaps the highest yield on this slide is the fact that the cleft, lip, and palate is due to failure of facial prominences to fuse.

6
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:32,620
Now remember that the way by which the face forms is that you have this prominence that grows from the top and then two that grow from the side and two that come from above.

7
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:42,520
And all of these five prominences, they fuse, and that eventually leads to formation of the face.

8
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:48,640
So if there's failure of those prominences to fuse, that can result in cleft, lip, and palate.

9
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,500
And so the high yield here is the fact that there's this embryology tie-in.

10
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:57,500
So I would remember that the facial prominences are very important in this particular disorder.

11
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:00,000
Here's what a cleft, lip, and palate looks like.

12
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:07,240
We're all familiar with this image, and this is a classic image here of a cleft lip, and underlying it is most likely a cleft palate.

13
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,500
Aphthous ulcer is a lesion that can occur on the oral mucosa.

14
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,220
It's a superficial ulceration that is painful.

15
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,640
It arises in relation to stress, often resolved spontaneously, but it can recur.

16
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:28,240
For anyone who has this or who experiences this, you know how painful it can be, and you realize that it will resolve and sometimes can recur.

17
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:33,420
It's characterized by a grayish base surrounded by erythema, and this is what it looks like.

18
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:38,480
You can clearly see that there's this grayish base, and it's surrounded by all of this erythema.

19
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,860
Now this grayish base actually represents granulation tissue.

20
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:51,840
One of the derivative syndromes concerning aphthous ulcers is Bissett syndrome, in which patients get recurrent aphthous ulcers plus genital ulcers and uveitis,

21
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:54,120
so that they may have some sort of visual disturbance.

22
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:59,800
So this is the triad, it's recurrent aphthous ulcers plus genital ulcers and uveitis.

23
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:04,800
It's actually due to immune complex vasculitis involving the small blood vessels.

24
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:06,580
We don't know the exact etiology.

25
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:11,280
It may be seen after a viral infection, but again, we don't know the exact etiology.

26
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,320
Oral herpes is when you have vesicles involving the oral mucosa.

27
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:20,300
These vesicles usually rupture, which then results in a shallow, painful red ulcer.

28
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:23,300
Important, it's usually due to HSV-1.

29
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,260
The primary infection with oral herpes occurs in childhood.

30
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:33,120
The lesion actually heals, but the virus remains dormant in the ganglia of the trigeminal nerve.

31
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,020
This is high yield.

32
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,620
Virus remains dormant in the ganglia of the trigeminal nerve.

33
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:42,000
Then what happens is that in the face of stress or sunlight or other types of stimuli,

34
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:46,440
you get reactivation of the virus, and the reactivation of the virus leads to vesicles

35
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,600
that usually arise on the lips and are classically called a cold sore.

36
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:52,560
And here's what a cold sore looks like.

37
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:54,740
It's this vesicle arising on the lips.

38
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,080
And again, that would be due to reactivation of HSV-1 virus,

39
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:03,380
which was remaining dormant in the ganglia of the trigeminal nerve.

40
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,040
Squamous cell carcinoma can also arise on the oral mucosa,

41
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:11,040
and this would be a malignant neoplasm of squamous cells, obviously.

42
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:14,720
Two major risk factors are going to be tobacco and alcohol.

43
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:18,060
Tobacco and alcohol, high yield, major risk factors.

44
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:22,720
The most common location within the oral mucosa is the floor of the mouth.

45
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,560
That's where we would most likely expect to see this cancer.

46
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:29,840
Now often, before you get squamous cell carcinoma, we can get dysplasia.

47
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,860
And when patients get squamous dysplasia, that often presents,

48
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:36,180
or it can present as leukoplakia or erythroplakia.

49
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:37,960
So these are precursor lesions.

50
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:40,540
Leukoplakia means white plaque.

51
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:42,800
Leuco means white, and plakia means plaque.

52
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:47,140
And when a patient has leukoplakia, you have to worry about squamous dysplasia.

53
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:52,200
Now, leukoplakia doesn't necessarily equal squamous cell dysplasia,

54
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:56,380
but it's something that you have to be concerned with when you see leukoplakia.

55
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:03,020
Basically, when you see leukoplakia, the way by which you kind of run through the workup in your mind

56
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:04,760
is that if you've got a patient with leukoplakia,

57
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:08,940
the very first thing that you might want to do is take a tongue depressor and try to scrape it away.

58
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:16,540
If you can scrape away that white plaque, then that indicates that you're dealing with oral candidiasis.

59
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:21,300
It's easily scraped away, and it's usually seen in immunocompromised patients.

60
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:26,040
Another thing that can give you a white plaque is something called hairy leukoplakia.

61
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:30,920
This is where you get a very shaggy, rough patch on the lateral tongue.

62
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:33,760
Lateral tongue is high yield, and that's important to know.

63
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:37,520
So, hairy leukoplakia is classically on the lateral aspect of the tongue,

64
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,020
and it gives you this rough, shaggy patch.

65
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:42,900
Now, important to know that it's due to EBV.

66
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,520
Again, important to know due to EBV.

67
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:48,100
Associated with an immunocompromised state,

68
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:50,540
usually seen in patients in an immunocompromised state,

69
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:53,960
and it only represents hyperplasia of the squamous mucosa.

70
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:55,540
There is no dysplasia there.

71
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:00,260
So, those are two things that you need to be able to distinguish from true leukoplakia.

72
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:03,220
That is, oral candidiasis and hairy leukoplakia.

73
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:05,040
When a patient has leukoplakia,

74
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,620
and if you're concerned that it might represent squamous dysplasia,

75
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:09,700
of course, a biopsy could be performed,

76
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:11,960
and you can do the biopsy to rule out carcinoma.

77
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:17,140
Now, erythroplakia refers to a red plaque, erythro, red, and plakia plaque.

78
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:18,780
When you have erythroplakia,

79
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:23,000
that indicates that you have vascularized leukoplakia,

80
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:25,440
meaning you have leukoplakia with a lot of blood vessels.

81
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:28,920
And that suggests that there might be some new blood vessel growth,

82
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:30,060
like angiogenesis,

83
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,940
and that would be highly suggestive of squamous dysplasia.

84
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:35,200
So, if I asked you which one of the following,

85
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:36,940
leukoplakia or erythroplakia,

86
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:39,440
is more suggestive of squamous dysplasia,

87
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:42,560
the answer would be erythroplakia.

88
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,700
So, that's very important to bear in mind.

89
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:48,100
Here's a classic picture of oral candidiasis.

90
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,580
This looks like a white kind of plaque,

91
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:53,440
but you would easily be able to scrape this away with a tongue depressor,

92
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,560
and the patient would clearly have a history of being in an immunocompromised state.

93
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:00,940
And that concludes this particular section on the oral mucosa.

94
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:02,980
The next section is the salivary gland.

